---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Stairs from '@/components/tools/calc2/Stairs.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="stair-calculator">
  <Stairs client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the stair calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The stair calculator divides the total rise (floor to floor) into equal risers no taller than the
      largest riser you allow. There is one tread fewer than risers, because the upper floor is the last
      step.
    </p>
    <pre><code>risers   n = ⌈total rise ÷ largest riser⌉
riser    r = total rise ÷ n
treads   n − 1
run      (n − 1) × tread depth
stringer √(total rise² + run²)
angle    arctan(r ÷ tread depth)
stride   2r + tread depth   (24 to 25 in is comfortable)</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A floor-to-floor height of 108 in with risers of at most 7¾ in needs ⌈108 ÷ 7.75⌉ = ⌈13.94⌉ = 14
      risers of 108 ÷ 14 = 7.714 in. With 10 in treads there are 13 treads and a total run of 130 in. The
      stringer is √(108² + 130²) = 169.0 in and the angle is arctan(7.714 ÷ 10) = 37.6°.
    </p>

    <h2>Code limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The International Residential Code allows risers up to 7¾ in and treads of at least 10 in, with 6 ft 8 in of headroom.</li>
      <li>Risers in one flight may differ by no more than ⅜ in, so all risers are equal here.</li>
      <li>Local codes differ. Commercial stairs have stricter limits (7 in risers, 11 in treads).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Measure the total rise from finished floor to finished floor. The stringer length is the line from
      floor to upper floor; the board you buy must be longer to allow for the cuts at each end.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
